-- title:
--   Theorem 4: $\frac{R(q^s,K^*)}{R(q^*)}\ge\frac{R(q^s,K^*)}{R_s(q^s)}\ge\frac{K}{N+K-1+\Lambda/\mu}$
-- statement:
--   Consider $N\ge1$ locations, $K$ vehicles, and an optimal solution $q^s$ of Problem (2) with workload budget $\Delta\ge K$. The static policy $q^s$ may split the locations into several disconnected networks; the $K$ vehicles are then allocated to the networks (Problem (8), p. 17). Let $\Lambda/\mu=\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A}\Lambda_{ij}/\mu_{ij}$ (Theorem 1, p. 7). Then:
--
--   1. every dynamic policy $q$ and every stationary distribution $\pi$ of its chain satisfy $R(q,\pi)\le R_s(q^s)$;
--   2. there is an allocation $(K_c)$ of the vehicles to the networks of $q^s$ such that the static chain has a stationary distribution concentrated on that allocation, and every such distribution $\pi$ satisfies
--   $$
--   \frac{K}{N+K-1+\Lambda/\mu}\,R_s(q^s)\le R(q^s,\pi);
--   $$
--   3. if $R_s(q^s)>0$, every such $\pi$ satisfies, with $R(q^*)$ the supremum of the rewards in item 1,
--   $$
--   \frac{R(q^s,\pi)}{R(q^*)}\ge\frac{R(q^s,\pi)}{R_s(q^s)}\ge\frac{K}{N+K-1+\Lambda/\mu}.
--   $$
--
--   This is Theorem 4: "$\frac{R(q^s,K^*)}{R(q^*)}\ge\frac{R(q^s,K^*)}{R_s(q^s)}\ge\frac{K}{N+K-1+\frac{\Lambda}{\mu}}$". Even when the optimal static prices disconnect the network, the static policy with an optimal allocation of vehicles earns a fraction at least $K/(N+K-1+\Lambda/\mu)$ of the optimal dynamic reward, uniformly in the number of networks.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - reading: R(q*), the reward of an optimal dynamic policy under its steady-state probability → Ropt, the supremum of R(q,π) over every dynamic policy q and every stationary distribution π of its chain; reason: a state-dependent policy can make the chain reducible, so π need not be unique; taking every stationary law makes R(q*) as large as possible, the stronger reading
--   - reading: R(q^s,K*), the reward of the static policy with the vehicles allocated by an optimal solution K* of Problem (8) → some allocation (K_c) with Σ K_c = K, a stationary law of the static chain concentrated on it, and the bound for every stationary law concentrated on it; reason: R(q^s,K*) is the maximum over allocations of the reward on the allocation class; a finite maximum is ≥ b iff some allocation's value is ≥ b, and quantifying over every stationary law on the class keeps the statement independent of uniqueness
--   - reading: q^s induces C connected networks with locations 𝒩_c, N_c and pairs 𝒜_c (p. 17) → the connected components of the graph with an edge {i,j} when Λij q^s_ij > 0 or Λji q^s_ji > 0; the transit queue of a pair with flow belongs to the network of its origin; queues without flow are empty on an allocation class; reason: every location lies in exactly one network (Σ N_c = N); under balanced flows weak and strong connectivity agree; queues without flow are transient under q^s
--   - generalization: C > 1 connected networks (§6) → any number C ≥ 1 of networks; reason: C = 1 is the setting of Theorem 1 and the proof covers it; the paper's case is a special case
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
--   - encoding: R(q^s,K*)/R(q*) ≥ R(q^s,K*)/Rs(q^s) ≥ K/(N+K−1+Λ/μ) → (a) R(q,π) ≤ Rs(q^s) for every policy and stationary law; (b) K/(N+K−1+Λ/μ)·Rs(q^s) ≤ R(q^s,π); (c) under 0 < Rs(q^s), both ratio inequalities with R(q*) = Ropt; reason: (a) is the content of the first inequality (Proposition 1); if Rs(q^s) = 0 then R(q*) = 0 and every ratio of the paper is 0/0, so the hypothesis of (c) excludes only undefined cases
--   - encoding: Λ = Σ_{(i,j)∈𝒜} Λij and 1/μ = Σ_{(i,j)∈𝒜} Λij/(Λμij) (Theorem 1, p. 7) → Λ/μ := Σ_{i,j} Λij·(1/μij); reason: equal to Λ·(1/μ) when Λ > 0; when Λ = 0 the paper's 1/μ is 0/0 and the sum is 0
--   - encoding: N locations, N ≥ 1 (implicit) → Nonempty P.L; reason: with no location and K ≥ 1 there is no state, so no stationary law and no allocation exist; the hypothesis excludes only that case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, Theorem 4, p. 18 (setup §6, p. 17; proof pp. 18–19)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsOptimal2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_ConcentratedOn
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_reward
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_Ropt
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_LamOverMu

namespace VehShare.MultiNet

/-- **Theorem 4: `R(q^s,K*)/R(q*) ≥ R(q^s,K*)/R_s(q^s) ≥ K/(N+K−1+Λ/μ)`.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), Theorem 4, p. 18 (setup §6, p. 17; proof pp. 18–19; Problem (2) with `Δ ≥ K`, pp. 5–6;
`Λ` and `1/μ` as in Theorem 1, p. 7).

Let `q^s` be optimal for Problem (2) with `Δ ≥ K`, on `N ≥ 1` locations. Then:
* (a) every dynamic policy `q` and every stationary distribution `π` of its chain earn
  `R(q, π) ≤ R_s(q^s)`;
* (b) there is an allocation `(K_c)` of the `K` vehicles to the networks of `q^s` such that the static
  chain has a stationary distribution concentrated on that allocation, and every such `π` earns
  `K/(N + K − 1 + Λ/μ) · R_s(q^s) ≤ R(q^s, π)`;
* (c) if `R_s(q^s) > 0`, every such `π` satisfies `R(q^s, π)/R_s(q^s) ≤ R(q^s, π)/R(q*)` and
  `K/(N + K − 1 + Λ/μ) ≤ R(q^s, π)/R_s(q^s)`, where `R(q*)` is the supremum of the rewards in (a).

**Formalization Note.** Reading 2 (`R(q*)` = `Ropt`, supremum over every policy and stationary
law); Reading 3 (`R(q^s, K*)` as "some allocation, a stationary law on it, every stationary law on
it", equivalent to the maximum of Problem (8)); any number `C ≥ 1` of networks (`C = 1` is Theorem 1's
second inequality) and no strong connectivity; `Λ/μ = ∑ Λ_ij/μ_ij` (`LamOverMu`); the hypothesis
`R_s(q^s) > 0` of (c) excludes only the case where every ratio of the paper is `0/0`; `Nonempty P.L`
excludes only the instance without locations. -/
theorem static_pricing_multi_network_bound (P : Instance) [Nonempty P.L] (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ)
    (hΔ : (K : ℝ) ≤ Δ) (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ) (hqs : IsOptimal2 P Δ qs) :
    (∀ (q : Policy P K) (π : State P K → ℝ), IsStationary q π → reward q π ≤ Rs P qs) ∧
    ∃ Kc : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent → ℕ, ∑ c, Kc c = K ∧
      (∃ π : State P K → ℝ,
        IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π ∧ ConcentratedOn P qs Kc π) ∧
      ∀ π : State P K → ℝ, IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π →
        ConcentratedOn P qs Kc π →
          (K : ℝ) / ((Fintype.card P.L : ℝ) + K - 1 + LamOverMu P) * Rs P qs ≤
              reward (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π ∧
            (0 < Rs P qs →
              reward (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π / Rs P qs ≤
                  reward (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π / Ropt P K ∧
                (K : ℝ) / ((Fintype.card P.L : ℝ) + K - 1 + LamOverMu P) ≤
                  reward (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1.1) π / Rs P qs) := by sorry

end VehShare.MultiNet
